lesson coding-square-rectangle-staircase v=3 format=teach grade=3 unit=3 subject=coding levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "A square, a rectangle, a staircase"
  goal "Tell a shape from its program, work out the distance all the way round and back again, and write a rectangle."

  look {
    scene 34x17 {
      row both at=canvas(1, 0) space=1 align=start {
        turtle sq cols=6 rows=6 col=1 row=6 moves=["right 4", "up 4", "left 4", "down 4"] mark=false
        turtle st cols=7 rows=7 col=1 row=7 moves=["right 2", "up 2", "right 2", "up 2", "right 2"] mark=false
      }
    }
    say """
      A square has four equal sides and comes back to the start; a rectangle has two pairs; a
      staircase never comes back at all. You can tell which is which from the numbers before you
      draw anything.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice coding.which-shape count=1 seed=4
    practice coding.shape-perimeter count=2 seed=4
    practice coding.perimeter-repeat count=2 seed=3
    show coding.missing-side w=6 h=2 easy-w=3 hard-w=8 hard-h=5
    show coding.missing-side w=4 h=3 easy-h=1 hard-w=9 hard-h=4
    practice coding.build-rectangle count=1 seed=2
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show coding.rectangle-bug w=4 h=2 k=1 easy-k=2 hard-w=3 hard-k=0
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show coding.rectangles-count s=8 easy-s=6 hard-s=12
  }
  remember {
    say "Add all four sides to go round a rectangle: two of one, two of the other."
  }

  grown-ups """
    This is the perimeter lesson from maths, arriving from the other side. If the child has met
    perimeter already, say so: it is the same adding up. A rectangle written with a repeat of two
    goes round the body twice, which is the step to check in the middle questions, and the missing
    side is the same sum worked backwards. In the two-star question the two forward numbers in
    the program are equal, so the drawing is a square; the dashed rectangle says which one should
    change. In the three-star question a list of pairs, starting 1 and 7, keeps the count honest.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the sides stay at four or less, the bug to find is a repeat that stops half way round, and the last question has three rectangles to list."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the rectangles written with a repeat have sides up to nine and no grid to count on, the wrong line draws a square only one square short of the target, and the last question has six rectangles to list."
  }
}
